WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.101
In the Letta framework,

2
00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.870
agents are designed to be deployed
as services.

3
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.807
In this lesson, we'll go over how
we can implement multi-agent collaboration

4
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:12.412
using tools for cross-agent communication
as well as sharing memory blocks.

5
00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.447
Let's have some fun!

6
00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:17.450
In the Letta framework,
agents are designed to run as a service

7
00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:20.787
so that a real application
can communicate with them via Rest APIs.

8
00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:24.290
So if you have something like a mobile app
that's using a chatbot,

9
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:27.160
that mobile app will send
Rest API requests

10
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.096
to some kind of Letta server
that's backed up by a database,

11
00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:33.800
and then that server will return back a
response that the mobile app can then use.

12
00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.537
So how can we have multi-agent
collaboration when we have agents

13
00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:38.872
running separate services?

14
00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.274
And we want them to coordinate
and communicate.

15
00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:45.111
So one solution is to have the agents
themselves send each other messages.

16
00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:45.912
So for example

17
00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:48.982
you could send a Post request
from an agent on one server to another.

18
00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:51.885
Another option is to have shared
memory blocks.

19
00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:54.888
So you can have blocks that are
in a shared persistent data store,

20
00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:59.059
and then have agents have synced to memory
blocks across different services.

21
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:03.329
In this lab, we're going to go over
how to implement multi-agent collaboration

22
00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.397
using Letta.

23
00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.367
We're going to do this
by essentially implementing a multi-agent

24
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:12.038
recruiting workflow for evaluating leads
and drafting personalized outreach emails.

25
00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.606
So we're going to have two different

26
00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:16.976
agents and evaluator agent
that evaluates lead candidates.

27
00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.947
And then also an outreach agent,
which draws personalized outreach emails.

28
00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:23.183
These two agents are going to be able
to communicate

29
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:26.186
and coordinate
with each other to work together.

30
00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.288
These agents will also have shared memory.

31
00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.925
We can essentially implement this
by creating a shared memory block

32
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.394
that is attached to both agents.

33
00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.930
And this shared memory
block will basically store memories

34
00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.700
about the company that the agents
are working together to recruit for.

35
00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:43.937
So as always, I'm going to

36
00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.906
create my Letta client
to connect to the running Letta service,

37
00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.410
and then I'm also going to paste
in a helper function

38
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.011
for printing the message.

39
00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.681
So we've used this already
in other lessons.

40
00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:58.051
But I'm also going to add in a print
for the type system message.

41
00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:01.855
And then also since we're streaming,
also make sure to print user statistics

42
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:04.858
when that's returned
back in the streaming messages.

43
00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:08.461
So before we create our agents
we're going to first create

44
00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:09.963
their shared memory block.

45
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:13.833
So this is basically a block that
we can create directly through the client

46
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.837
that will be attached to both agents
so that the agents can have shared memory.

47
00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:22.308
So this block is basically going to have
a value which is the company description.

48
00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.710
So the company is called

49
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:27.947
Agent OS and is deploying AI tools to make
it easier to create and deploy LLM agents.

50
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:31.050
And then we can use the client
to create this block.

51
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.920
So we'll pass in as the value
the company description

52
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.589
and then also have the block,
have the label company,

53
00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.858
and then also
a limit of 10,000 characters.

54
00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:41.861
So I can print out this

55
00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:45.465
return block object to see that it also
now has this unique ID.

56
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:49.969
So we can now reference this block
either to modify or to retrieve it

57
00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.939
later on using this unique
block ID with the client.

58
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.009
So now let's create our two agents.

59
00:02:56.009 --> 00:03:00.613
So in this setting we're going to create
both an outreach agent for drafting emails

60
00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:04.450
and then also an evaluation agent
for evaluating different candidates.

61
00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.587
The first station,
which is going to be the outreach

62
00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:10.757
agent, is going to have access
to a draft candidate email tool.

63
00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:15.528
So this is basically a tool to write out
an email draft in the contact field

64
00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:18.765
so that we can later
review it or decide to send it.

65
00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.769
So we'll create this tool
with the same upsert from function tool

66
00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.571
that we've used with the client before it.

67
00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:27.774
And so this will basically instantiate
a Letta tool on the server.

68
00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.977
And now we can create our outreach agent.

69
00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:33.546
So we're going to give it
basically a persona that kind of gives it

70
00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.548
some descriptions
about how it should be acting.

71
00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:39.352
So we say in the persona
you're responsible for drafting emails

72
00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:42.956
on behalf of a company with the draft
candidate email tool. Candidates to

73
00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.457
email will be messaged to you.

74
00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.459
So we'll kind of go over this
a bit more later.

75
00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:49.996
But strong candidates will be sent to this
agent from the other agent,

76
00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:53.299
so that this outreach agent
can draft an email for that candidate.

77
00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:57.136
So, we are now
also creating the agent from the client.

78
00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:59.505
So we'll pass in the name Outreach Agent.

79
00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.007
We're also pass in one memory

80
00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:04.410
block for the persona
that has the label persona and the value.

81
00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:08.781
And then we're actually also attaching
the block that we created before.

82
00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:12.719
So we don't actually pass in the block
here because the block has already

83
00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:13.620
been created.

84
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.224
We instead pass in the block
ID to tell Letta that this block ID

85
00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:21.561
that we previously made should be attached
to this agent, and the data

86
00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:24.731
inside of this block should be contained
within this agent's context window.

87
00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:28.134
And then of course, we also set the LLM
model in the embedding model.

88
00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:30.436
And then also pass in this tool name.

89
00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.773
You can also do this
by passing in the tool ID in the tool IDs.

90
00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:37.377
So next we're
going to create the evaluation agent.

91
00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.279
So this agent will have a tool

92
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.481
which is a reject tool
so it can reject the candidate.

93
00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.349
So decide not to email a candidate.

94
00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:46.719
And we're going to create this reject tool
first, so we can attach it to the agent.

95
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.356
And for this agent
we're going to construct a persona for it.

96
00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:54.127
So the eval persona that tells it
we are looking for this set of skills.

97
00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:57.263
So things like front-end, software
engineering experience with our LLMs.

98
00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:00.233
So ideal
candidate should have these skills.

99
00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:02.468
And these weaker candidates
that don't match

100
00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.971
the skill sets
should go through the reject tool

101
00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:08.775
and strong candidate should be sent
to the agent with the ID of the outreach

102
00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.143
agent that we just created.

103
00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.646
So by passing in the outreach
agents ID into the persona,

104
00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:17.684
we're telling this agent which other agent
should you message in order to

105
00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:21.421
communicate that this candidate is strong
and we want to draft an email for them.

106
00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:25.058
So now
we can actually create the eval agent.

107
00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:28.094
So similarly to the other agent,
we're just going to provide

108
00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:31.431
one memory block for the persona with that
Eval persona we just made

109
00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:35.168
defining the model and the LLM model
and the embedding model.

110
00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:37.136
We're attaching the reject tool.

111
00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:40.540
So this time we're just passing it in with
ID you can again also do this with name.

112
00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:43.876
And then we're passing in by name
the send message to agent

113
00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.478
and wait for reply tool.

114
00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:48.481
So this tool is actually
a Letta built-in tool.

115
00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:52.919
It allows the agents to message
other agents running on the same server

116
00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:56.089
by specifying a message to send,
and then also the id.

117
00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:59.692
So this evaluation will know
the ID of the other outreach agent

118
00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:02.328
because we pass it
the ID into the persona.

119
00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:05.098
You can also actually define tools
like this yourself.

120
00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:09.469
So in previous labs we've defined tools
that connect to the client and interact

121
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:10.069
with the client.

122
00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:10.803
So you can of course

123
00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:15.208
create your own custom
cross messaging tool by using the client.

124
00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:18.378
But Letta also provide some,
which is what we're using here.

125
00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:22.115
And then we're actually also going to set
include based tools to false.

126
00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:26.052
So like we mentioned
previously Letta agents will by default

127
00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:29.822
have a bunch of tools related
to sending messages memory management etc.

128
00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:32.291
but for simplification
we're just going to set this to false.

129
00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:35.094
So this agent is more limited in scope.

130
00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:37.997
And then like the other agent,
we're also going to attach the company

131
00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:41.033
block IDs so that this information
is in its context window.

132
00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:45.705
And then finally we're actually going
to specify something called tool rules.

133
00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:48.775
So this is kind of a way to constrain
the agent's behavior.

134
00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:52.412
In this case we're asking the agent
to basically exit the loop.

135
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:54.180
So to terminate execution

136
00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.316
after it calls the send message to agent
and wait for reply tool.

137
00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.686
So this basically ensures
that we don't get the agent looping.

138
00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:03.856
Once the agent does
send that message to the other agent, it stops.

139
00:07:05.358 --> 00:07:08.361
So you can see that because we set
include based tools to false,

140
00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:11.731
this agent actually only has two tools
the reject tool and the send message

141
00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:14.734
to agent and wait for reply tool.

142
00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:17.937
So now we can actually send
some resume data to the agents.

143
00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:21.874
So we're going to read this resume
from the local file system for candidate

144
00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:22.942
name Tony Stark.

145
00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:24.544
Then we're

146
00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:27.647
going to send this resume data
to the eval agent directly.

147
00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:30.616
So we're going to send this message
to evaluation with this ID

148
00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:32.652
and provide the content.

149
00:07:32.652 --> 00:07:35.855
Just please evaluate this resume
with the resume data that we just read.

150
00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.225
And so we can see that the agent reasons

151
00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:42.261
it's evaluating Tony Stark's profile
seem strong.

152
00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:46.699
And so then the agent decides to send the
agent wait for reply to this other agent.

153
00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:49.702
So provides information
about the candidate name

154
00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:51.938
and any other information
that might be relevant

155
00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:53.973
and it decides
to send this to the other agent

156
00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:58.973
with this agent ID of the outreach agent.
So it knows this other agent ID,

157
00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.680
because we provide it in the persona,

158
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.616
so it's able to reach out
to this other agent,

159
00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:06.953
and we can see that this agent
actually returns back a response.

160
00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.823
So it's able to call the send message tool
and return back this response, which is

161
00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:11.958
this drafted email.

162
00:08:12.959 --> 00:08:15.328
And so
we get back this tool return response.

163
00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:18.798
And then finally
we print out the total usage statistics.

164
00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:23.536
So this is step count one
since the evaluation only made one step.

165
00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:25.271
But there is of course another step

166
00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:28.274
that was made by the other agent
that was doing outreach.

167
00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:32.078
We can of course
also see the list of messages

168
00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:35.081
or the events that happened
from the outreach agent side.

169
00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:37.416
So to do this,
we can list out the messages

170
00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:40.419
for the outreach agent
ID we're going to skip the first message

171
00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:44.290
since this message is basically just a
system prompt sent to the LLM provider.

172
00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:47.393
So the other messages are kind of
what's actually in this history.

173
00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:51.664
And so you can see that
there's a couple of messages here.

174
00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:54.066
These are basically what we call
the initial message sequence.

175
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:56.702
To kind of give some in-context
examples to the agent.

176
00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:58.704
You can of course changes if you like.

177
00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:01.173
And so we see here
that there's a system message.

178
00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:04.510
So this is basically a message
that this agent received

179
00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:08.314
from the other agent that has this
incoming message from this agent ID,

180
00:09:09.782 --> 00:09:13.085
and also provides a hint that this agent
should make sure to call send message

181
00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.388
at the end, reply back to this agent,

182
00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.925
and then we see this content about Tony
Stark that was sent by the other agent.

183
00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:23.829
And so after it gets this alert, the agent
decides to call draft candidate email.

184
00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:28.801
So it drafts this email and then
it returns back this drafted email.

185
00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:33.472
And then also sends this message as a send
message tool back to the other agent.

186
00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:34.907
So you can see that here.

187
00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:36.842
It's writing the send message.

188
00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:39.845
And so this was what was actually shared
to the other agent.

189
00:09:41.647 --> 00:09:45.585
So these agents also have a shared memory
block which we created at the beginning.

190
00:09:45.718 --> 00:09:48.654
So what this means is that if
either agent edits this block

191
00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:51.657
then those changes will be reflected
on both agents.

192
00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:55.161
So for example we can send a message
to the outreach agent basically

193
00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:58.364
telling it, you know, the company
has actually rebranded the Letta

194
00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:00.833
from Agent OS.

195
00:10:00.833 --> 00:10:04.637
And so the agent basically calls
the core memory replace memory editing

196
00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:09.375
tool to replace Agent OS with Letta
and confirms that and sends a message.

197
00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:13.012
And so, unsurprisingly,
now if we retrieve the memory

198
00:10:13.012 --> 00:10:17.283
block of the eval agent, it now says Letta
instead of Agent OS.

199
00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:20.353
But if we also retrieve the memory block

200
00:10:20.353 --> 00:10:23.356
of the outreach agent.

201
00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.094
Then we can see that this also says now,
even though the outreach agent did

202
00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:31.664
not edit its memory, its memory now
says that the company is called Letta.

203
00:10:31.931 --> 00:10:35.668
And this is again because each of
these blocks is actually the same ID.

204
00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:38.838
So they both
have you can see here like matching IDs.

205
00:10:39.405 --> 00:10:42.241
And so both of these agents,
their context windows or in context

206
00:10:42.241 --> 00:10:45.244
memory is synchronized
to the same memory block.

207
00:10:46.045 --> 00:10:49.849
So we just went over how you can create
multi-agent coordination through tools.

208
00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:52.752
But often simpler way to do
this is to actually use

209
00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:55.755
the multi-agent group abstraction
inside of Letta.

210
00:10:56.255 --> 00:10:58.824
So before we get the setup,
we're actually going to once again

211
00:10:58.824 --> 00:11:01.827
load in a print message multi-agent tool.

212
00:11:01.861 --> 00:11:04.296
So this is very similar
to what we had before.

213
00:11:04.296 --> 00:11:07.533
But we're actually going to also print out
the message dot name field.

214
00:11:07.767 --> 00:11:09.335
So in a multi-agent setting there's

215
00:11:09.335 --> 00:11:11.837
obviously multiple agents
that are talking.

216
00:11:11.837 --> 00:11:15.408
So we use the name field to distinguish
between multiple agents which will

217
00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:16.375
have different names.

218
00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.612
So we're now going to recreate our agents.

219
00:11:19.612 --> 00:11:22.248
So this is exactly the same agents
as before.

220
00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:24.950
Except this time
we don't need to provide any tooling

221
00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.952
to allow the agents
to chat with each other.

222
00:11:26.952 --> 00:11:31.123
So you might recall before that we had
a special send message to other agent tool

223
00:11:31.323 --> 00:11:34.293
that allowed the eval
agent to communicate with outreach agent.

224
00:11:34.293 --> 00:11:36.295
But we're not going to use tools
this time.

225
00:11:36.295 --> 00:11:39.298
We're just going to create
both these agents separately.

226
00:11:39.331 --> 00:11:43.169
And now we can place both of these agents
into a multi-agent group.

227
00:11:43.436 --> 00:11:46.138
So this is a very simple multi-agent
example.

228
00:11:46.138 --> 00:11:50.643
We're basically just doing round robin
between each of these agents in order.

229
00:11:51.077 --> 00:11:53.746
But this basically
will group the agents together.

230
00:11:53.746 --> 00:11:56.649
And so they'll have a single group chat.

231
00:11:56.649 --> 00:11:59.018
So a single message history
that's all shared.

232
00:11:59.018 --> 00:12:02.988
So both agents can see the other agents
messages as well as user messages

233
00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:04.423
in a single thread,

234
00:12:04.423 --> 00:12:07.893
as opposed to in the other example,
each agent has its own thread.

235
00:12:08.127 --> 00:12:10.963
But they communicate to each other
via tools.

236
00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.965
So let's now try reading another resume.

237
00:12:12.965 --> 00:12:15.968
So basically a resume
for a SpongeBob SquarePants.

238
00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:19.205
And so now we can actually
message the group with this content.

239
00:12:19.472 --> 00:12:22.975
So the group messaging a group
is very similar to messaging an agent.

240
00:12:22.975 --> 00:12:25.010
It's almost exactly the same API,

241
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.980
except the only difference is instead
of specifying an agent ID,

242
00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.815
we're just specifying a group ID.

243
00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:32.151
So we're basically
just grabbing this round-robin group

244
00:12:32.151 --> 00:12:34.153
and then passing in its ID.

245
00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:36.522
And we're going to send this message
which is a user message.

246
00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:38.057
And then just the content of the resume.

247
00:12:38.057 --> 00:12:40.826
So very similar to before,
except we're just messaging a group.

248
00:12:42.128 --> 00:12:44.930
So we're now printing
out the response stream from the group.

249
00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:47.767
And you can see here
that we're printing the name of the agent.

250
00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:50.870
So basically who said this
or who had this reasoning message.

251
00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:55.341
So we can see that the eval decides
to reject SpongeBob SquarePants.

252
00:12:55.508 --> 00:12:58.310
And again this is all kind of
in the same group chat.

253
00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.313
So both agents can see messages in here.

254
00:13:01.413 --> 00:13:04.083
And so this evaluation completes.

255
00:13:04.083 --> 00:13:06.051
And then this outreach agent
actually decides

256
00:13:06.051 --> 00:13:09.021
to draft a rejection email
to SpongeBob SquarePants.

257
00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:12.691
So again, because this agent
is able to see the entire message

258
00:13:12.691 --> 00:13:16.829
history of the other agent,
it decides to use this draft email tool.

259
00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:20.366
And then it sends out this message
and then does reasoning.

260
00:13:20.366 --> 00:13:23.702
And then finally the outreach agent
also sends a final message,

261
00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:26.705
which is just telling us that it draft
a rejection email.

262
00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:30.643
And at the very end, we get the usage
statistics of what all the agents did.

263
00:13:31.343 --> 00:13:34.780
You can maybe prompt this to get
the outreach agent to not send an email

264
00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:38.851
at the end, but in this case, the agent
decided to use this tool anyways.

265
00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.321
This is
just another way to do multi-agent.

266
00:13:42.321 --> 00:13:45.391
Depending on your use case,
you may want the agents to share

267
00:13:45.391 --> 00:13:48.394
a group chat, or you may want them
to have separate threads

268
00:13:48.394 --> 00:13:51.363
and only communicate when necessary.

269
00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:52.431
So congratulations!

270
00:13:52.431 --> 00:13:56.168
You've now created a multi-agent
collaborative setting and Letta

271
00:13:56.368 --> 00:13:57.336
in two different ways,

272
00:13:57.336 --> 00:14:01.173
both with having a group chat
and then also through multi-agent tools.

273
00:14:01.407 --> 00:14:03.776
You've also created agents
with shared memory

274
00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:06.779
so that they can share memories
about the same concepts,

275
00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:10.449
whether it's an organization or a user,
or other things that you might want to add

276
00:14:10.449 --> 00:14:11.150
to your agents.

